  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Run a certified OpenID Provider inside your own ASP.NET Core application instead of operating a separate identity product. Abblix OIDC Server is a library: it builds on the standard .NET dependency injection container, stores nothing you do not choose to store, and passes the OpenID Foundation conformance suite in every login and logout profile. What you get: authorization code with PKCE, hybrid and implicit flows, client credentials, device authorization, CIBA, token exchange (RFC 8693) and Rich Authorization Requests (RFC 9396); pushed authorization requests, signed and encrypted request objects, JARM responses and sender-constrained tokens through DPoP or mutual TLS; dynamic client registration and management, discovery and Authorization Server Metadata (RFC 8414), token introspection and revocation, front-channel and back-channel logout, session management. New in 3.0: multi-tenancy, with tenants declared in the settings or kept in a store of the host's own and changed while the server runs; every JWT validation states the token types it accepts (RFC 8725); a client authentication JWT names the issuer as its only audience (RFC 10070); tracing and metrics; per-client budgets on introspection and revocation; a default scope per client and the resources each client may get an access token for; a refresh token reuse policy; and a host step before anything is issued. It runs on .NET 11. Host it through the MVC or the Minimal API integration package. Full details: https://github.com/Abblix/Oidc.Server/releases/tag/v3.0",
